    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old male named John with moderate dementia is brought to the clinic by his grandson for a routine checkup. During physical examination, you note multiple linear, purple-blue bruises on his inner arms and chest. When asked how these injuries occurred, the grandson immediately interrupts, stating, ''John is clumsy and fell out of bed a few days ago.'' The patient remains silent, hypervigilant, and avoids eye contact. What is the most appropriate next step in clinical management?', '["Interview the patient alone in a safe, private space to conduct a targeted screening for physical abuse and neglect, and file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) based on reasonable suspicion", "Confront the caregiver aggressively in front of the patient, accusing them of domestic violence and physical assault", "Advise the caregiver to use soft physical restraints at night to prevent the patient from falling out of bed", "Reassure the caregiver that minor falls are common in dementia and prescribe a daily low-dose aspirin for cardiovascular protection", "Order a full-body skeletal survey and head CT to document all injuries before talking to either the patient or caregiver"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Physical abuse in older adults is characterized by non-accidental physical force resulting in injury. Bruises on non-bony, protected areas (such as the back, chest, inner arms, or thighs) are highly red-flagged for intentional trauma, as accidental falls typically injure bony prominences (knees, elbows, forehead). The initial step is always to interview the patient alone to ensure safety and allow for non-judgmental, direct screening. Clinicians are legally mandated to report any reasonable suspicion of elder abuse or neglect to Adult Protective Services (APS). Absolute proof is not required. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
