    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old female named Martha with severe dementia is brought to the clinic by her paid caregiver for a routine checkup. During physical examination, you note multiple linear, purple-blue bruises on her upper back and chest. When asked how these injuries occurred, the paid caregiver immediately interrupts, stating, ''Martha is clumsy and fell out of bed a few days ago.'' The patient remains silent, hypervigilant, and avoids eye contact. What is the most appropriate next step in clinical management?', '["Reassure the caregiver that minor falls are common in dementia and prescribe a daily low-dose aspirin for cardiovascular protection", "Order a full-body skeletal survey and head CT to document all injuries before talking to either the patient or caregiver", "Advise the caregiver to use soft physical restraints at night to prevent the patient from falling out of bed", "Confront the caregiver aggressively in front of the patient, accusing them of domestic violence and physical assault", "Interview the patient alone in a safe, private space to conduct a targeted screening for physical abuse and neglect, and file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) based on reasonable suspicion"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Physical abuse in older adults is characterized by non-accidental physical force resulting in injury. Bruises on non-bony, protected areas (such as the back, chest, inner arms, or thighs) are highly red-flagged for intentional trauma, as accidental falls typically injure bony prominences (knees, elbows, forehead). The initial step is always to interview the patient alone to ensure safety and allow for non-judgmental, direct screening. Clinicians are legally mandated to report any reasonable suspicion of elder abuse or neglect to Adult Protective Services (APS). Absolute proof is not required. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old female named Esther with severe dementia is brought to the clinic by her spouse for a routine checkup. During physical examination, you note multiple linear, purple-blue bruises on her inner arms and chest. When asked how these injuries occurred, the spouse immediately interrupts, stating, ''Esther is clumsy and fell out of bed a few days ago.'' The patient remains silent, hypervigilant, and avoids eye contact. What is the most appropriate next step in clinical management?', '["Reassure the caregiver that minor falls are common in dementia and prescribe a daily low-dose aspirin for cardiovascular protection", "Advise the caregiver to use soft physical restraints at night to prevent the patient from falling out of bed", "Interview the patient alone in a safe, private space to conduct a targeted screening for physical abuse and neglect, and file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) based on reasonable suspicion", "Confront the caregiver aggressively in front of the patient, accusing them of domestic violence and physical assault", "Order a full-body skeletal survey and head CT to document all injuries before talking to either the patient or caregiver"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Physical abuse in older adults is characterized by non-accidental physical force resulting in injury. Bruises on non-bony, protected areas (such as the back, chest, inner arms, or thighs) are highly red-flagged for intentional trauma, as accidental falls typically injure bony prominences (knees, elbows, forehead). The initial step is always to interview the patient alone to ensure safety and allow for non-judgmental, direct screening. Clinicians are legally mandated to report any reasonable suspicion of elder abuse or neglect to Adult Protective Services (APS). Absolute proof is not required. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old male named Henry with severe dementia is brought to the clinic by his spouse for a routine checkup. During physical examination, you note multiple linear, purple-blue bruises on his inner arms and chest. When asked how these injuries occurred, the spouse immediately interrupts, stating, ''Henry is clumsy and fell out of bed a few days ago.'' The patient remains silent, hypervigilant, and avoids eye contact. What is the most appropriate next step in clinical management?', '["Confront the caregiver aggressively in front of the patient, accusing them of domestic violence and physical assault", "Interview the patient alone in a safe, private space to conduct a targeted screening for physical abuse and neglect, and file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) based on reasonable suspicion", "Advise the caregiver to use soft physical restraints at night to prevent the patient from falling out of bed", "Reassure the caregiver that minor falls are common in dementia and prescribe a daily low-dose aspirin for cardiovascular protection", "Order a full-body skeletal survey and head CT to document all injuries before talking to either the patient or caregiver"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Physical abuse in older adults is characterized by non-accidental physical force resulting in injury. Bruises on non-bony, protected areas (such as the back, chest, inner arms, or thighs) are highly red-flagged for intentional trauma, as accidental falls typically injure bony prominences (knees, elbows, forehead). The initial step is always to interview the patient alone to ensure safety and allow for non-judgmental, direct screening. Clinicians are legally mandated to report any reasonable suspicion of elder abuse or neglect to Adult Protective Services (APS). Absolute proof is not required. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old male named Frank is brought to the emergency department in a state of hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS) with a blood glucose of 850 mg/dL. His niece, who manages Frank''s medications, states he has been administering his insulin daily. However, the patient''s pharmacy records reveal that his insulin prescriptions have not been refilled for the past 6 months, despite Frank having adequate financial insurance. Physical examination shows severe dehydration, poor hygiene, and a soiled diaper. Which type of elder abuse is best represented in this scenario?', '["Self-neglect; which occurs when an older adult refuses or fails to manage their own essential personal care and safety", "Physical abuse; which involves the intentional infliction of physical pain, injury, or bodily impairment", "Active caregiver neglect; which involves the deliberate withholding of essential life-sustaining care, medications, or nutrition by a caregiver", "Passive caregiver neglect; which occurs when a well-meaning caregiver is overwhelmed and lacks the knowledge or capacity to provide care", "Financial exploitation; which involves the illegal or improper use of an elder''s funds, assets, or property"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Active caregiver neglect is the intentional withholding of food, medication, medical care, or hygiene services by a designated caregiver. In this case, the caregiver deliberately withheld life-sustaining insulin (indicated by the unfilled prescriptions over several months despite insurance), resulting in life-threatening hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS). This contrasts with passive neglect, where the caregiver lacks the capacity or resources but has no malicious or deliberate intent to withhold care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old male named Charles is brought to the emergency department in a state of hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS) with a blood glucose of 900 mg/dL. His grandson, who manages Charles''s medications, states he has been administering his insulin daily. However, the patient''s pharmacy records reveal that his insulin prescriptions have not been refilled for the past 3 months, despite Charles having adequate financial insurance. Physical examination shows severe dehydration, poor hygiene, and a soiled diaper. Which type of elder abuse is best represented in this scenario?', '["Active caregiver neglect; which involves the deliberate withholding of essential life-sustaining care, medications, or nutrition by a caregiver", "Self-neglect; which occurs when an older adult refuses or fails to manage their own essential personal care and safety", "Physical abuse; which involves the intentional infliction of physical pain, injury, or bodily impairment", "Passive caregiver neglect; which occurs when a well-meaning caregiver is overwhelmed and lacks the knowledge or capacity to provide care", "Financial exploitation; which involves the illegal or improper use of an elder''s funds, assets, or property"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Active caregiver neglect is the intentional withholding of food, medication, medical care, or hygiene services by a designated caregiver. In this case, the caregiver deliberately withheld life-sustaining insulin (indicated by the unfilled prescriptions over several months despite insurance), resulting in life-threatening hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS). This contrasts with passive neglect, where the caregiver lacks the capacity or resources but has no malicious or deliberate intent to withhold care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old female named Louise is brought to the emergency department in a state of hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS) with a blood glucose of 650 mg/dL. Her adult son, who manages Louise''s medications, states she has been administering her insulin daily. However, the patient''s pharmacy records reveal that her insulin prescriptions have not been refilled for the past 4 months, despite Louise having adequate financial insurance. Physical examination shows severe dehydration, poor hygiene, and a soiled diaper. Which type of elder abuse is best represented in this scenario?', '["Passive caregiver neglect; which occurs when a well-meaning caregiver is overwhelmed and lacks the knowledge or capacity to provide care", "Active caregiver neglect; which involves the deliberate withholding of essential life-sustaining care, medications, or nutrition by a caregiver", "Financial exploitation; which involves the illegal or improper use of an elder''s funds, assets, or property", "Physical abuse; which involves the intentional infliction of physical pain, injury, or bodily impairment", "Self-neglect; which occurs when an older adult refuses or fails to manage their own essential personal care and safety"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Active caregiver neglect is the intentional withholding of food, medication, medical care, or hygiene services by a designated caregiver. In this case, the caregiver deliberately withheld life-sustaining insulin (indicated by the unfilled prescriptions over several months despite insurance), resulting in life-threatening hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS). This contrasts with passive neglect, where the caregiver lacks the capacity or resources but has no malicious or deliberate intent to withhold care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
