    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old female named Martha is brought to the emergency department in a state of hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS) with a blood glucose of 720 mg/dL. Her spouse, who manages Martha''s medications, states she has been administering her insulin daily. However, the patient''s pharmacy records reveal that her insulin prescriptions have not been refilled for the past 5 months, despite Martha having adequate financial insurance. Physical examination shows severe dehydration, poor hygiene, and a soiled diaper. Which type of elder abuse is best represented in this scenario?', '["Active caregiver neglect; which involves the deliberate withholding of essential life-sustaining care, medications, or nutrition by a caregiver", "Self-neglect; which occurs when an older adult refuses or fails to manage their own essential personal care and safety", "Physical abuse; which involves the intentional infliction of physical pain, injury, or bodily impairment", "Passive caregiver neglect; which occurs when a well-meaning caregiver is overwhelmed and lacks the knowledge or capacity to provide care", "Financial exploitation; which involves the illegal or improper use of an elder''s funds, assets, or property"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Active caregiver neglect is the intentional withholding of food, medication, medical care, or hygiene services by a designated caregiver. In this case, the caregiver deliberately withheld life-sustaining insulin (indicated by the unfilled prescriptions over several months despite insurance), resulting in life-threatening hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS). This contrasts with passive neglect, where the caregiver lacks the capacity or resources but has no malicious or deliberate intent to withhold care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old male named Thomas is brought to the emergency department in a state of hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS) with a blood glucose of 720 mg/dL. His nephew, who manages Thomas''s medications, states he has been administering his insulin daily. However, the patient''s pharmacy records reveal that his insulin prescriptions have not been refilled for the past 4 months, despite Thomas having adequate financial insurance. Physical examination shows severe dehydration, poor hygiene, and a soiled diaper. Which type of elder abuse is best represented in this scenario?', '["Passive caregiver neglect; which occurs when a well-meaning caregiver is overwhelmed and lacks the knowledge or capacity to provide care", "Physical abuse; which involves the intentional infliction of physical pain, injury, or bodily impairment", "Active caregiver neglect; which involves the deliberate withholding of essential life-sustaining care, medications, or nutrition by a caregiver", "Self-neglect; which occurs when an older adult refuses or fails to manage their own essential personal care and safety", "Financial exploitation; which involves the illegal or improper use of an elder''s funds, assets, or property"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Active caregiver neglect is the intentional withholding of food, medication, medical care, or hygiene services by a designated caregiver. In this case, the caregiver deliberately withheld life-sustaining insulin (indicated by the unfilled prescriptions over several months despite insurance), resulting in life-threatening hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS). This contrasts with passive neglect, where the caregiver lacks the capacity or resources but has no malicious or deliberate intent to withhold care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old male named Walter with advanced Parkinson''s disease and severe mobility impairment is brought to the clinic by his elderly spouse. On examination, the patient has severe perineal dermatitis, deep skin folds filled with dried urine and feces, and an unaddressed sacral pressure injury. The spouse appears extremely exhausted, tearful, and admits, ''I am doing my best, but Walter has become too heavy for me to lift, and I can''t keep up with the laundry or bathing.'' There are no signs of intentional physical trauma. What is the most appropriate primary diagnosis and clinical approach?', '["Self-neglect; refer the patient to an involuntary psychiatric facility for treatment of cognitive decline", "Passive caregiver neglect; provide immediate nursing care, engage social work to set up home health aids and respite care, and file a mandatory report with Adult Protective Services (APS)", "Active caregiver neglect; immediately notify law enforcement to arrest the spouse for criminal abandonment", "Medical non-adherence; instruct the spouse that they must perform physical therapy at home for 4 hours daily", "Institutional abuse; report the spouse to the state long-term care ombudsman for administrative review"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Passive caregiver neglect occurs when a caregiver fails to provide necessary care due to physical, financial, or cognitive limitations, or severe caregiver burnout, without deliberate intent to cause harm. The spouse is clearly overwhelmed by the physical demands of caregiving. While the clinician must still file a mandatory report to Adult Protective Services (APS) to mobilize supportive resources, the management should focus on helper support: arranging home health services, physical therapy, respite care, and social work assistance to reduce caregiver burden. (Ref: Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
