    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old female named Phyllis with advanced Parkinson''s disease and severe mobility impairment is brought to the clinic by her elderly spouse. On examination, the patient has severe perineal dermatitis, deep skin folds filled with dried urine and feces, and an unaddressed sacral pressure injury. The spouse appears extremely exhausted, tearful, and admits, ''I am doing my best, but Phyllis has become too heavy for me to lift, and I can''t keep up with the laundry or bathing.'' There are no signs of intentional physical trauma. What is the most appropriate primary diagnosis and clinical approach?', '["Active caregiver neglect; immediately notify law enforcement to arrest the spouse for criminal abandonment", "Institutional abuse; report the spouse to the state long-term care ombudsman for administrative review", "Passive caregiver neglect; provide immediate nursing care, engage social work to set up home health aids and respite care, and file a mandatory report with Adult Protective Services (APS)", "Self-neglect; refer the patient to an involuntary psychiatric facility for treatment of cognitive decline", "Medical non-adherence; instruct the spouse that they must perform physical therapy at home for 4 hours daily"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Passive caregiver neglect occurs when a caregiver fails to provide necessary care due to physical, financial, or cognitive limitations, or severe caregiver burnout, without deliberate intent to cause harm. The spouse is clearly overwhelmed by the physical demands of caregiving. While the clinician must still file a mandatory report to Adult Protective Services (APS) to mobilize supportive resources, the management should focus on helper support: arranging home health services, physical therapy, respite care, and social work assistance to reduce caregiver burden. (Ref: Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old female named Jean with advanced dementia is accompanied to the clinic by her spouse. The clinic social worker notices that the patient''s bank recently flagged several large, cash ATM withdrawals and a sudden transfer of Jean''s home title to the spouse. During a private interview, Jean appears confused and states, ''My spouse told me I had to sign some papers to keep receiving my social security checks, but I don''t know what they were.'' The patient has no signs of physical trauma. What is the most appropriate diagnostic classification and next step?', '["Psychological abuse; immediately prescribe a high-dose atypical antipsychotic to treat the patient''s paranoia about the spouse", "Financial exploitation; document the clinical interview meticulously, file a mandatory report with Adult Protective Services (APS), and advise the family to contact financial institutions and elder law services", "Self-neglect; advise the patient to immediately close all bank accounts and keep cash under her mattress", "Systemic institutional abuse; contact the state long-term care ombudsman to audit the bank''s transaction security", "Medical neglect; order a stat non-contrast head CT and toxicology screening to rule out acute encephalopathy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Financial exploitation involves the illegal, unauthorized, or improper use of an older adult''s resources, funds, property, or assets for personal gain. Red flags include large unexplained bank withdrawals, signing over deeds/titles under coercion, and changes to wills or powers of attorney in the setting of cognitive impairment. Clinicians are legally mandated to report suspected financial exploitation to Adult Protective Services (APS). Supportive measures include consulting elder law attorneys and notifying banks. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 96-year-old male named Paul with mild dementia is accompanied to the clinic by his daughter. The clinic social worker notices that the patient''s bank recently flagged several large, cash ATM withdrawals and a sudden transfer of Paul''s home title to the daughter. During a private interview, Paul appears confused and states, ''My daughter told me I had to sign some papers to keep receiving my social security checks, but I don''t know what they were.'' The patient has no signs of physical trauma. What is the most appropriate diagnostic classification and next step?', '["Financial exploitation; document the clinical interview meticulously, file a mandatory report with Adult Protective Services (APS), and advise the family to contact financial institutions and elder law services", "Psychological abuse; immediately prescribe a high-dose atypical antipsychotic to treat the patient''s paranoia about the daughter", "Self-neglect; advise the patient to immediately close all bank accounts and keep cash under his mattress", "Systemic institutional abuse; contact the state long-term care ombudsman to audit the bank''s transaction security", "Medical neglect; order a stat non-contrast head CT and toxicology screening to rule out acute encephalopathy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Financial exploitation involves the illegal, unauthorized, or improper use of an older adult''s resources, funds, property, or assets for personal gain. Red flags include large unexplained bank withdrawals, signing over deeds/titles under coercion, and changes to wills or powers of attorney in the setting of cognitive impairment. Clinicians are legally mandated to report suspected financial exploitation to Adult Protective Services (APS). Supportive measures include consulting elder law attorneys and notifying banks. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
