    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old female named Jean with advanced dementia is accompanied to the clinic by her adult son. The clinic social worker notices that the patient''s bank recently flagged several large, cash ATM withdrawals and a sudden transfer of Jean''s home title to the adult son. During a private interview, Jean appears confused and states, ''My adult son told me I had to sign some papers to keep receiving my social security checks, but I don''t know what they were.'' The patient has no signs of physical trauma. What is the most appropriate diagnostic classification and next step?', '["Systemic institutional abuse; contact the state long-term care ombudsman to audit the bank''s transaction security", "Financial exploitation; document the clinical interview meticulously, file a mandatory report with Adult Protective Services (APS), and advise the family to contact financial institutions and elder law services", "Psychological abuse; immediately prescribe a high-dose atypical antipsychotic to treat the patient''s paranoia about the adult son", "Medical neglect; order a stat non-contrast head CT and toxicology screening to rule out acute encephalopathy", "Self-neglect; advise the patient to immediately close all bank accounts and keep cash under her mattress"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Financial exploitation involves the illegal, unauthorized, or improper use of an older adult''s resources, funds, property, or assets for personal gain. Red flags include large unexplained bank withdrawals, signing over deeds/titles under coercion, and changes to wills or powers of attorney in the setting of cognitive impairment. Clinicians are legally mandated to report suspected financial exploitation to Adult Protective Services (APS). Supportive measures include consulting elder law attorneys and notifying banks. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old male named Bernard lives alone and is brought to the clinic by a concerned neighbor. The neighbor reports that Bernard''s house is filled with trash, has no running water or heat, and multiple dogs are roaming inside. On examination, Bernard is cachectic, disheveled, has long unkempt nails, and smells of urine. Cognitive screening reveals a Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA) score of 14/30. Bernard adamantly refuses any assistance, stating, ''I am fine, I have lived here for 40 years, and I don''t want anyone in my house.'' What is the most appropriate clinical interpretation and action?', '["Respect the patient''s autonomy and discharge him home immediately with no further action, as competent adults have the right to live as they choose", "Unilaterally sign the patient over to a long-term care nursing home without performing a capacity assessment", "Involuntarily admit the patient to a secure psychiatric unit for lifetime management of hoarding disorder", "Diagnose self-neglect; perform a comprehensive capacity assessment to determine if he has the capacity to refuse services, and file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS)", "Contact law enforcement to have the patient arrested for public nuisance and animal cruelty"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Self-neglect is the most common form of elder abuse reported to APS. It occurs when an older adult fails to provide themselves with adequate food, shelter, hygiene, or medical care, posing a threat to their health and safety. In patients who refuse assistance, the clinician must assess **decision-making capacity**. If the patient lacks capacity (e.g., due to advanced cognitive decline, represented by a low MoCA score), protective interventions can be forced via legal channels (emergency guardianship). If the patient retains capacity, they have the legal right to refuse services, but APS should still be involved to offer voluntary support and harm reduction. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
