eatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old male named Carl is seen in the clinic for chronic hypertension. During the visit, his paid caregiver constantly belittles him, calling him ''stupid,'' ''dirty,'' and a ''burden'' in front of the staff. The paid caregiver repeatedly states, ''If you don''t start behaving, I will lock you in the basement or put you in the worst nursing home in the state.'' Carl sits with head bowed, trembling, and whispers, ''I''m sorry.'' There are no physical bruises. What is the most accurate description of this behavior and the clinician''s obligation?', '["Psychological or emotional abuse; which involves the infliction of anguish, pain, or distress through verbal or non-verbal acts; the clinician is legally required to report this to Adult Protective Services (APS)", "Passive caregiver neglect; refer the caregiver to a local clinical training program for anger management, but do not report the case", "Normal caregiver-patient interaction; reassure the caregiver that their frustration is completely justified and offer no further interventions", "Self-neglect; advise the patient to seek outpatient psychotherapy to improve his self-esteem and ignore the caregiver''s comments", "Physical abuse; file a report with local law enforcement to immediately arrest the caregiver for physical assault"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Psychological or emotional abuse in older adults includes verbal assaults, threats of abuse, harassment, intimidation, infantilization, and social isolation. While it does not leave physical marks, it causes significant mental anguish and is associated with increased mortality in older adults. Clinicians must identify these interactions, document them objectively in the medical record, interview the patient alone, and file a mandatory report with Adult Protective Services (APS). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
