    (v_topic_id, 'A 96-year-old male named Joseph is seen in the clinic for chronic hypertension. During the visit, his paid caregiver constantly belittles him, calling him ''stupid,'' ''dirty,'' and a ''burden'' in front of the staff. The paid caregiver repeatedly states, ''If you don''t start behaving, I will lock you in the basement or put you in the worst nursing home in the state.'' Joseph sits with head bowed, trembling, and whispers, ''I''m sorry.'' There are no physical bruises. What is the most accurate description of this behavior and the clinician''s obligation?', '["Self-neglect; advise the patient to seek outpatient psychotherapy to improve his self-esteem and ignore the caregiver''s comments", "Passive caregiver neglect; refer the caregiver to a local clinical training program for anger management, but do not report the case", "Normal caregiver-patient interaction; reassure the caregiver that their frustration is completely justified and offer no further interventions", "Physical abuse; file a report with local law enforcement to immediately arrest the caregiver for physical assault", "Psychological or emotional abuse; which involves the infliction of anguish, pain, or distress through verbal or non-verbal acts; the clinician is legally required to report this to Adult Protective Services (APS)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Psychological or emotional abuse in older adults includes verbal assaults, threats of abuse, harassment, intimidation, infantilization, and social isolation. While it does not leave physical marks, it causes significant mental anguish and is associated with increased mortality in older adults. Clinicians must identify these interactions, document them objectively in the medical record, interview the patient alone, and file a mandatory report with Adult Protective Services (APS). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old male named William with severe, non-verbal dementia is brought from a long-term care facility to the emergency department due to acute agitation and a change in behavior. On physical examination, the nurse notes unexplained bruising on the patient''s inner thighs, perineal area, and new-onset vaginal bleeding. Direct examination reveals lacerations of the posterior fourchette. Which type of elder abuse is suspected, and what is the most critical immediate clinical action?', '["Suspected systemic physical abuse; prescribe an atypical antipsychotic to manage the patient''s agitation and return him to the facility", "Suspected passive neglect; counsel the nursing home administrator on upgrading their staff training regarding diaper changes", "Suspected medical neglect; schedule a follow-up appointment with a gynecologist in 4 weeks to re-evaluate the bleeding", "Suspected self-neglect; advise the facility staff to use adult diapers and apply topical zinc oxide ointment to the perineum", "Suspected elder sexual abuse; perform a thorough, gentle medical-forensic examination (rape kit if within time window), secure the patient''s immediate safety, and immediately notify law enforcement and the state health department/ombudsman"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Elder sexual abuse is defined as non-consensual sexual contact of any kind with an older adult, particularly those who lack the capacity to consent (such as patients with advanced, non-verbal dementia). Red flags include perineal/inner thigh bruising, unexplained genital bleeding, vaginal lacerations, and sudden behavioral changes. Immediate steps include ensuring the patient''s safety, performing a medical-forensic exam by a trained specialist (SANE), documenting all injuries, and immediately reporting the case to both law enforcement and the state regulatory agencies (such as the Long-Term Care Ombudsman/Department of Health for institutional settings). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
