    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-year-old male named Harry with moderate Alzheimer''s disease is residing in a local nursing home. During an unannounced visit, his family finds Harry tied to a wheelchair with a bedsheet and heavily sedated. The nursing staff states, ''We are short-staffed today, and Harry kept wandering toward the exit, so we had to give him extra diphenhydramine and tie him down to prevent a fall.'' There is no physician order for restraints or sedation. How should this scenario be classified and managed?', '["Appropriate fall prevention; praise the nursing staff for their proactive safety measures and document the restraint as standard nursing care", "Self-neglect; advise the family to hire a private duty nurse to sit with the patient, as the facility is not responsible for exit-seeking behavior", "Institutional abuse and neglect; the use of physical restraints (tying with a sheet) and chemical restraints (sedation) for staff convenience or as a substitute for supervision is illegal; file a report with the Long-Term Care Ombudsman and state licensing department", "Medical neglect by the physician; file a complaint against the medical director for failing to prescribe daily physical restraints", "Physical abuse by the family; accuse the family of failing to provide a safe home environment, forcing the patient into a nursing home"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Institutional abuse refers to abuse or neglect that occurs in a residential facility (nursing home, assisted living). The use of **physical restraints** (e.g., tying a patient to a chair) or **chemical restraints** (e.g., administering sedatives like diphenhydramine, benzodiazepines, or antipsychotics) for the convenience of staff or as a substitute for active supervision is strictly prohibited by federal and state regulations (OBRA 1987). Clinicians must report this immediately to the **Long-Term Care Ombudsman** and the state health department, who oversee institutional standards of care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old female named Ruth with severe osteoarthritis and chronic open-angle glaucoma is brought to the clinic. You note that the patient has missed 4 consecutive ophthalmology appointments over the past year. The caregiver, her granddaughter, admits to not filling the patient''s prostaglandin analog eye drops because ''it''s too much driving, and she is old anyway.'' On exam, the patient is found to have progressed to near-total blindness in the left eye. What is the clinical classification of this finding?', '["Medical neglect; which occurs when a caregiver fails to provide the necessary medical care, treatments, or medications required to maintain the elder''s physical health, leading to preventable harm", "Active physical abuse; because the caregiver intentionally damaged the patient''s optic nerve using physical trauma", "Accidental age-related decline; since vision loss is an inevitable part of normal aging and cannot be prevented", "Self-neglect; because the patient should have driven themselves to the pharmacy and eye clinic", "Financial exploitation; because the caregiver saved money by not buying the eye drops"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Medical neglect is a subtype of neglect where a caregiver fails to provide necessary medical treatments, medications, or appointments to an older adult who is dependent on them, resulting in physical deterioration or injury. Withholding glaucoma medication and failing to bring the patient to critical specialist appointments, leading to permanent, preventable blindness, is a classic case of medical neglect. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old male named Frank with severe osteoarthritis and chronic open-angle glaucoma is brought to the clinic. You note that the patient has missed 5 consecutive ophthalmology appointments over the past year. The caregiver, his granddaughter, admits to not filling the patient''s prostaglandin analog eye drops because ''it''s too much driving, and he is old anyway.'' On exam, the patient is found to have progressed to near-total blindness in the left eye. What is the clinical classification of this finding?', '["Medical neglect; which occurs when a caregiver fails to provide the necessary medical care, treatments, or medications required to maintain the elder''s physical health, leading to preventable harm", "Accidental age-related decline; since vision loss is an inevitable part of normal aging and cannot be prevented", "Financial exploitation; because the caregiver saved money by not buying the eye drops", "Active physical abuse; because the caregiver intentionally damaged the patient''s optic nerve using physical trauma", "Self-neglect; because the patient should have driven themselves to the pharmacy and eye clinic"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Medical neglect is a subtype of neglect where a caregiver fails to provide necessary medical treatments, medications, or appointments to an older adult who is dependent on them, resulting in physical deterioration or injury. Withholding glaucoma medication and failing to bring the patient to critical specialist appointments, leading to permanent, preventable blindness, is a classic case of medical neglect. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
