    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old female named Lois with severe osteoarthritis and chronic open-angle glaucoma is brought to the clinic. You note that the patient has missed 3 consecutive ophthalmology appointments over the past year. The caregiver, her daughter, admits to not filling the patient''s prostaglandin analog eye drops because ''it''s too much driving, and she is old anyway.'' On exam, the patient is found to have progressed to near-total blindness in the left eye. What is the clinical classification of this finding?', '["Accidental age-related decline; since vision loss is an inevitable part of normal aging and cannot be prevented", "Active physical abuse; because the caregiver intentionally damaged the patient''s optic nerve using physical trauma", "Self-neglect; because the patient should have driven themselves to the pharmacy and eye clinic", "Medical neglect; which occurs when a caregiver fails to provide the necessary medical care, treatments, or medications required to maintain the elder''s physical health, leading to preventable harm", "Financial exploitation; because the caregiver saved money by not buying the eye drops"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Medical neglect is a subtype of neglect where a caregiver fails to provide necessary medical treatments, medications, or appointments to an older adult who is dependent on them, resulting in physical deterioration or injury. Withholding glaucoma medication and failing to bring the patient to critical specialist appointments, leading to permanent, preventable blindness, is a classic case of medical neglect. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old male named William with advanced dementia is found sitting alone in the emergency department waiting room. He is wearing soiled clothes and has a note pinned to his jacket written by his paid caregiver stating, ''I cannot do this anymore. I am leaving the state. Please take care of him.'' The patient is unable to provide his address or the caregiver''s contact information. What is the correct term for this form of abuse, and what is the immediate clinical action?', '["Abandonment; admit the patient to the hospital for safety, perform a comprehensive medical evaluation, and immediately notify Adult Protective Services (APS) and law enforcement", "Medical neglect; prescribe a cholinesterase inhibitor to restore the patient''s memory so he can go home alone", "Self-neglect; contact a local homeless shelter to arrange overnight lodging and discharge the patient from the ED", "Passive neglect; instruct the emergency department security staff to search the nearby parking lot and force the caregiver to return", "Institutional abuse; report the emergency department administration to the state health department for patient dumping"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Abandonment is the desertion of an older adult by an individual who has assumed responsibility for providing care or physical custody. It is a severe form of elder abuse. In this scenario, leaving an incapacitated patient in an ED waiting room without a safe handoff constitutes abandonment. Immediate management involves ensuring the patient''s safety, admitting them to the hospital (as they cannot be safely discharged), performing a comprehensive medical assessment, and filing a report with both Adult Protective Services (APS) and law enforcement to locate the caregiver. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
